## FIX: skip SET STATEMENT_TIMEOUT on SEA — the real "Error querying graph" cause

Context: The retry above did NOT fix "Error querying graph" — it only delayed
the error (3 attempts, ~10s) because the SEA `HTTP 500` was **persistent, not a
cold-start transient**. Bisecting the connector path against the live RT
warehouse ("Reyen Engine", REYDEN, serverless) isolated the exact trigger:

- `use_sea=True` + `use_cloud_fetch=False` + a `SET STATEMENT_TIMEOUT` statement
  → SEA `HTTP 500` (reproducible even with `SET STATEMENT_TIMEOUT` alone).
- Same connection, two plain `SELECT`s, or the aggregate query on its own → OK.
- With cloud-fetch ON, `SET` works — so the fault is the deprecated SEA backend
  mishandling `SET` statements under the inline (non-external-links) disposition.

The graph read path (`GraphDBBackend.get_aggregate_stats` etc. via
`DeltaFlatStore.execute_query`) always runs the bounded variant, which issues
`SET STATEMENT_TIMEOUT = N` before the query and `= 0` after. On a Lakehouse/RT
warehouse with cloud-fetch disabled, that first `SET` 500s every time, so the
Explorer stats/filter calls returned `502 Error querying graph` on every load.
The identical statement submitted over the REST Statement Execution API
SUCCEEDS, confirming the fault is in the connector's SEA backend, not the query
or the warehouse.

Fix: in `SQLWarehouse.execute_query()`, disable the server-side statement bound
when the connection uses SEA (`self._auth.use_sea`) — skip both `SET
STATEMENT_TIMEOUT` statements and rely on the client-side `_socket_timeout`.
Classic Thrift warehouses are unchanged and keep the server-side bound. The
retry helper from the previous section is retained as genuine defence-in-depth
for real transient transport errors (actual cold-starts), but is no longer the
mechanism that makes RT graph queries work.

Changes:
1. src/back/core/databricks/SQLWarehouse.py
   `execute_query()` now sets `bounded = False` when
   `getattr(self._auth, "use_sea", False)` is truthy (with a DEBUG log), before
   building the work closure — so no `SET STATEMENT_TIMEOUT` is emitted on SEA.
2. tests/units/core/test_sql_warehouse.py
   Added `TestSeaStatementTimeout`: asserts `execute_query(..., statement_timeout_s=30)`
   emits only the query on a `use_sea=True` auth (no `STATEMENT_TIMEOUT`), and
   still emits `SET STATEMENT_TIMEOUT = 30/0` on a Thrift (`use_sea=False`) auth.

