## PERF: stop async graph handlers from freezing the event loop

Context: After the SEA fix, RT graph queries succeeded but the KG/Explorer was
slow to show first results, and unrelated panels loaded on the same page
(mapping, ontology, registry) all stalled ~12s in lock-step. Root cause: several
`async def` handlers in `src/api/routers/internal/dtwin.py` called blocking SQL
directly on the event-loop thread. A single serverless-warehouse query
(`/sync/stats` runs four sequential COUNT/COUNT-DISTINCT queries over ~7.6M
triples, ~5–12s) blocked the loop for its full duration, so every concurrent
request the page fired queued behind it. The individual queries are genuinely
slow (serverless RT + `COUNT(DISTINCT)` over millions of rows), but the freeze
made the whole UI unresponsive rather than just that one panel.

`/sync/filter` (the two-phase search that produces the first results) already
offloaded via `run_blocking`, so the delay was mostly it being stuck behind the
event-loop-blocking `/sync/stats`.

Fix: offload the blocking SQL sections of the remaining `async` graph handlers
to the sized thread pool (`run_blocking`), matching the pattern already used by
`/sync/filter`, `/sync/info`, `/clusters/detect`, etc. The queries are unchanged
(still as slow individually), but they no longer stall the event loop, so the
page paints panels as each response lands and the perceived "time to first
results" drops sharply.

Changes:
1. src/api/routers/internal/dtwin.py
   - `triplestore_stats` (`GET /sync/stats`): moved the four blocking store
     calls + payload assembly into a nested `_compute_stats()` run via
     `await run_blocking(_compute_stats)`.
   - `load_triplestore` (`POST /sync/load`): `store.query_triples(...)` now via
     `run_blocking`.
   - `dtwin_triples_find` (`GET /triples/find`): `DigitalTwin.find_triples_bfs`
     now via `run_blocking` (multi-round-trip BFS).
   - `dtwin_neighbors` (`GET /neighbors`): the per-hop `expand_entity_neighbors`
     loop + `get_triples_for_subjects` bulk fetch moved into a nested
     `_expand()` run via `run_blocking` (right-click "Expand neighbours").

