## PERF: make `/sync/stats` genuinely fast (parallel round-trips + approx distinct)

Context: Follow-up to the event-loop fix above. The stats panel was no longer
blocking, but still slow: it fired four blocking warehouse round-trips
*sequentially*, so its latency was the *sum* of them, and the aggregate query
paid for an exact `COUNT(DISTINCT subject)` — a full distinct shuffle over
~7.6M-row triple tables on the serverless RT (Reyen) warehouse. This addresses
the two speedups flagged in the previous section.

Changes:
1. src/api/routers/internal/dtwin.py
   - `triplestore_stats` (`GET /sync/stats`): the four independent store calls
     (`get_aggregate_stats`, `get_type_distribution`, `get_predicate_distribution`,
     `get_inferred_triple_count`) now run concurrently in the thread pool via
     `asyncio.gather(run_blocking(...), ...)` instead of one sequential
     `run_blocking(_compute_stats)`. Total latency drops from ~sum to ~max of the
     four. CPU-only assembly (predicate classification, the cheap analytics-job
     check) still runs on the loop afterwards; the
     `analytics_job_configured(domain, settings)` call is kept on one line to
     satisfy the source-text assertion in `test_analytics_job_status.py`.
2. src/back/core/graphdb/GraphDBBackend.py
   - New `_distinct_count_expr(column)` hook (default exact `COUNT(DISTINCT ...)`)
     used by `get_aggregate_stats` for the subject/predicate distinct counts, so
     backends can override the cardinality strategy without touching the query.
3. src/back/core/graphdb/delta/DeltaFlatStore.py
   - Override `_distinct_count_expr` to use Spark's `approx_count_distinct`
     (HyperLogLog): O(1) memory, no distinct shuffle, turning the multi-second
     aggregate query into sub-second on large graphs. ~5% relative error is
     acceptable for an at-a-glance overview. Lakebase/Postgres keeps the exact
     count (it has no built-in approximate counter and its graphs are OLTP-sized).

Note on "lazy-load stats after graph renders" (third flagged item): already the
case — stats is fetched by `loadInsights()` on the Insights section, a separate
XHR from the Explorer graph load (`query-sigmagraph.js` → `/sync/filter`), so the
graph never waits on stats. No frontend change needed.

