## UX: show the search response time on the graph canvas

Context: To make the Explorer's latency visible, show how long a search took —
from the click to the graph appearing on the canvas — as a small readout pinned
to the bottom-left of the canvas.

Changes:
1. src/front/static/query/js/query-sigmagraph.js
   - Added a pause/resume stopwatch (`_searchTimer{Start,Pause,Resume,Stop}` over
     `_searchElapsedMs` / `_searchTimerRunning` / `_searchTimerPending`). The
     clock starts on the Search click (`_executeGraphSearch`), pauses when the
     seed-selection popup opens, resumes on the "Explore" click
     (`_expandSelectedSeeds`), and stops when the graph is rendered — so the
     readout is click→display minus the user's selection dwell. The single-result
     path (no popup) is timed straight through.
   - `_render` closes the clock once the graph is on the canvas and calls the new
     `_showSearchTiming(seconds)`, which renders a "⏱ N.NNs" pill (absolute,
     bottom-left, `#sgSearchTiming`) anchored to `#sgContainer`. Only
     search-driven renders update it; filter/group toggles leave it untouched,
     and an empty (0-node) result clears the pending flag so no stale time shows.

